From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 07:19:14 +0900
Subject: Let quoted code refer to the members a class defined in the quote
 inherits

A class defined in a quote could not reliably use the members of its parent.

A reference to a protected member from quoted code gets an accessor, since the
quote may be spliced where the member is not accessible, unless it is made
through the `this` of a class defined in the quote, which inherits the member
wherever it ends up. That exception looked for a selection `this.m`, but a
member referred to by its name alone is typed as an identifier, so it never
applied to one. The accessor was then entered in the quoted class after its
body had been typed, and the expansion failed with "undefined:
this.inline$m"; or, where a quote nested in a splice had already made the
accessor, the accessor's own body was rewritten to call itself, and the
expanded program did not terminate. The exception now covers identifiers.

A reference from a quote nested in a splice to something defined in the
enclosing quote is passed into the splice as an argument of its hole. What
counted as defined in the quote was decided by the symbol alone, so a member
which a class of the quote only inherits was left in the nested quote as it
was, with the `this` of a class that does not exist where that quote is
pickled: "unresolved symbols: anonymous class". Such a reference is now
captured as one to a member the class defines itself is, whether it is read,
applied or assigned to. It is recorded under the reference and not the
member's symbol, because two classes of the quote may inherit the same member.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: quotedthis
